import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;

public class VulnerableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb";
        String user = "username";
        String password = "password";

        try {
            // This is vulnerable because it directly concatenates user input into the query.
            Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);
            System.out.println("Connected successfully!");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

### Good Code Snippet

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;

public class SecureExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb";
        String user = "username";
        String password = "password";

        try {
            // Use PreparedStatement for parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection.
            String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
            Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);

            PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql);
            pstmt.setString(1, "admin"); // Parameterized query to avoid SQL injection.

            ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();

            while (rs.next()) {
                System.out.println(rs.getString("name"));
            }

            pstmt.close();
            conn.close();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In the bad code snippet, the user input (`"admin"` in this case) is directly concatenated into the SQL query string, which makes it susceptible to SQL injection attacks if the input comes from untrusted sources. The good code snippet uses prepared statements, which help protect against such attacks by separating the SQL logic from the data being used.

